    {
      "id": "OXZdPwGiygf4PejwCHCEF",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1479.9403682425425,
      "y": -466.26540404938646,
      "width": 737,
      "height": 1950,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 50,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0F",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 157974978,
      "version": 2550,
      "versionNonce": 1084627666,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1795256090620,
      "created": 1794615265154,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "User sends -> payments.example.com to dns and gets ip for\npayments.example.com.\n1st-> TCP Handshake\n2nd-> TLS Handshake\n3rd-> HTTP request/response\n\n-> User/browser sends hello to server.\n-> server signs the certificate OF HANDSHAKE TRANSCRIPT** with\nprivatekey and sends back cert+ public key(is inside cert).\n    -> Sends certificate and proves possession of private key by\nsigning handshake data.\n-> Brower/Client verifies the signed data and cert and TLS\nHandshake complete.\n\n\nOpenSSL - cryptographic command line tool - used to generate\nprivate/public key\n\n- We generate the private and public key on our end - using OpenSSL\n- Generate CSR(certificate signing request) and submit to\ncertificate Authority(CA)\n    - payments.csr\n        -> openssl req -new \\\n          -key private.key \\\n          -out payments.csr \\\n          -subj \"/CN=payments.example.com\"\n    \n    - CSR Contains : Domain/Identity Info | Public Key | Signature\nmade using Private Key[**Currently Generated(server) Private key**]\n\n- CA Creates the signed X.509 certificate(bind domain with public\nkey). - signs the cert using [**CA's Private key**]\n\n- Configure LB with cert and private key or ACM manages it ->\n    - certificate.crt\n    - private.key\n\n- Step1 -> Client Hello: \n    Supported TLS versions\n    Supported cipher suites\n    Client random\n    Key share (for ECDHE)\n    SNI = payments.example.com -> tells ALB which host the client\nwant to access  \n\n- Step2 -> server Hello + Certificate:\n\n    Selected TLS version\n    Selected cipher suite\n    Server random\n    Key share\n\nCertificate (ALB → Browser)\nSends X.509 certificate containing the public key and CA signature.\nCertificate was previously signed by the CA.\n\nStep 3: \n\nCertificateVerify (ALB → Browser)\nALB signs the handshake transcript using its private key.\nProves possession of the private key corresponding to the\ncertificate's public key.\n\n\nStep 4: Browser verifies everything\nThe browser performs two separate verifications.\n\nA. Certificate verification\n- Is the certificate signed by a trusted CA?\n- Is the certificate expired?\n- Does the hostname match payments.example.com?\n- Is the certificate chain valid?\n- Are there other certificate validity issues?\nB. CertificateVerify verification\n- Extract the public key from the certificate.\n- Verify the signature over the handshake transcript.\n- Confirm that the signature is valid.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "gFssN01iX2VjtLSmWzLbJ",
      "originalText": "User sends -> payments.example.com to dns and gets ip for payments.example.com.\n1st-> TCP Handshake\n2nd-> TLS Handshake\n3rd-> HTTP request/response\n\n-> User/browser sends hello to server.\n-> server signs the certificate OF HANDSHAKE TRANSCRIPT** with privatekey and sends back cert+ public key(is inside cert).\n    -> Sends certificate and proves possession of private key by signing handshake data.\n-> Brower/Client verifies the signed data and cert and TLS Handshake complete.\n\n\nOpenSSL - cryptographic command line tool - used to generate private/public key\n\n- We generate the private and public key on our end - using OpenSSL\n- Generate CSR(certificate signing request) and submit to certificate Authority(CA)\n    - payments.csr\n        -> openssl req -new \\\n          -key private.key \\\n          -out payments.csr \\\n          -subj \"/CN=payments.example.com\"\n    \n    - CSR Contains : Domain/Identity Info | Public Key | Signature made using Private Key[**Currently Generated(server) Private key**]\n\n- CA Creates the signed X.509 certificate(bind domain with public key). - signs the cert using [**CA's Private key**]\n\n- Configure LB with cert and private key or ACM manages it ->\n    - certificate.crt\n    - private.key\n\n- Step1 -> Client Hello: \n    Supported TLS versions\n    Supported cipher suites\n    Client random\n    Key share (for ECDHE)\n    SNI = payments.example.com -> tells ALB which host the client want to access  \n\n- Step2 -> server Hello + Certificate:\n\n    Selected TLS version\n    Selected cipher suite\n    Server random\n    Key share\n\nCertificate (ALB → Browser)\nSends X.509 certificate containing the public key and CA signature.\nCertificate was previously signed by the CA.\n\nStep 3: \n\nCertificateVerify (ALB → Browser)\nALB signs the handshake transcript using its private key.\nProves possession of the private key corresponding to the certificate's public key.\n\n\nStep 4: Browser verifies everything\nThe browser performs two separate verifications.\n\nA. Certificate verification\n- Is the certificate signed by a trusted CA?\n- Is the certificate expired?\n- Does the hostname match payments.example.com?\n- Is the certificate chain valid?\n- Are there other certificate validity issues?\nB. CertificateVerify verification\n- Extract the public key from the certificate.\n- Verify the signature over the handshake transcript.\n- Confirm that the signature is valid.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
