    },
    {
      "id": "YC9yUMiOr0URFgGZKpROJ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2338.645644168316,
      "y": -573.6973291873451,
      "width": 880,
      "height": 750,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 40,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0Y",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 812316318,
      "version": 195,
      "versionNonce": 1288128614,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1795253545940,
      "created": 1794649926549,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Step 1: ECDHE — establish shared secret material\n\nBoth browser and server contribute ephemeral key-exchange values.\n\nThey independently derive the same shared secret.\n\nAn eavesdropper observing the exchange cannot feasibly calculate that secret.\n\nStep 2: HKDF — derive encryption keys\n\nThe shared secret is input to HKDF (which uses HMAC internally).\n\nHKDF derives separate traffic keys for each direction:\nShared secret material\n        |\n        v\n       HKDF\n        |\n        +----> Client write key\n        |\n        +----> Server write key\n\n\nStep 3: AES-GCM — encrypt HTTP data\n\nNow the browser encrypts its HTTP application data using its client traffic key.\n\nThe server decrypts it using its corresponding client traffic key.\n\nThe server uses a separate traffic key for data it sends back.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "fMIpUS3BIB_o-xtxolR-5",
      "originalText": "Step 1: ECDHE — establish shared secret material\n\nBoth browser and server contribute ephemeral key-exchange values.\n\nThey independently derive the same shared secret.\n\nAn eavesdropper observing the exchange cannot feasibly calculate that secret.\n\nStep 2: HKDF — derive encryption keys\n\nThe shared secret is input to HKDF (which uses HMAC internally).\n\nHKDF derives separate traffic keys for each direction:\nShared secret material\n        |\n        v\n       HKDF\n        |\n        +----> Client write key\n        |\n        +----> Server write key\n\n\nStep 3: AES-GCM — encrypt HTTP data\n\nNow the browser encrypts its HTTP application data using its client traffic key.\n\nThe server decrypts it using its corresponding client traffic key.\n\nThe server uses a separate traffic key for data it sends back.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
